1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,072
Your death was scheduled for Tuesday at precisely noon, and I am the one who wrote it.

2
00:00:06,372 --> 00:00:13,884
Every morning, my newsroom terminal prints names of healthy people who will vanish before the week ends.

3
00:00:14,184 --> 00:00:21,528
We never question the system. We just describe how they fall, turning breathing souls into past tense.

4
00:00:21,828 --> 00:00:30,060
My editor says the ink is sacred. Once a draft is finalized, the universe clears its calendar.

5
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:38,088
Today I sat down with my coffee, expecting a stranger's tragedy. Instead, my own keystrokes betrayed me.

6
00:00:38,388 --> 00:00:46,308
My address. My birthdate. Even the absurd detail about the chipped coffee mug sitting right beside my keyboard.

7
00:00:46,608 --> 00:00:53,208
If I delete the file, do I cheat the clock? Or does the empty screen mean the end comes faster?

8
00:00:53,508 --> 00:01:01,020
My fingers refused to click. The cursor moved on its own, finishing the final sentence without my permission.

9
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:09,024
The period at the end of my story flashed green. Outside my window, all the city streetlights suddenly died.

10
00:01:09,324 --> 00:01:16,404
The screen went dark except for one blinking cursor waiting for a new author. Who types the words now?
